<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/customers-and-markets">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Markets are <strong>groups of people with a shared problem and budget</strong> — and
    strategy begins by choosing which group to serve and how to be remembered by it. The
    working sequence has a name, <strong>STP</strong>: <em>segment</em> the market by
    reason-to-buy, <em>target</em> the segment you can serve best, <em>position</em> your
    offer on a specific place in that segment's mind. Product–market fit is the verdict on
    whether the sequence worked. Everything in this page is upstream of advertising: get
    STP right and even modest promotion converts; get it wrong and no campaign saves you.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="segment, target, position — and how to know when it worked" widget="equationStep" config={custCfg} />

  <h2>What marketing is actually for</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> marketing means advertising — the persuasive bit you
    see. In the syllabus sense, marketing is the whole discipline of <strong>identifying,
    anticipating and satisfying customer needs profitably</strong>; advertising is one tool
    at the end of a much longer chain. The job starts with understanding the market: who
    buys, why, what they'd switch for, and what the competition offers. Two measurements
    anchor it. <strong>Market size</strong> (total sales volume or value) tells you the
    prize; <strong>market share</strong> (your sales ÷ market sales) tells you your
    standing — and is often the single most-watched number in a boardroom because share
    tends to compound: bigger firms buy cheaper, advertise harder and appear in more
    places. Markets also never sit still — incomes rise, tastes shift, technology
    substitutes, new competitors appear — so "the market" is a moving photograph that must
    be re-taken continuously. Kodak's film market didn't shrink because people stopped
    wanting pictures; it shrank because the <em>job</em> moved to a different technology.
  </p>

  <h2>Competition sets the rules of the game</h2>
  <p>
    Marketing freedom depends on the market's structure, and economists name four cases.
    In <strong>perfect competition</strong> (wheat, commodities), products are identical and
    nobody has pricing power — marketing is nearly pointless, and profit margins sit at
    subsistence. In <strong>monopolistic competition</strong> — restaurants, clothing,
    most consumer markets — many sellers offer <em>differentiated</em> versions of a similar
    thing: differentiation is exactly what buys you pricing power, which is why branding
    exists at all (bottled water is chemically similar and commercially very different).
    An <strong>oligopoly</strong> — a few big players (aircraft makers, supermarkets,
    mobile networks) — plays by watchfulness: every move is matched, price wars destroy
    everyone, so competition shifts to advertising, loyalty schemes and product features.
    A <strong>monopoly</strong> can ignore the customer until a regulator or a substitute
    intervenes. The practical reading for a new entrant: find where structure is soft
    (fragmented, undifferentiated, low loyalty) and enter with a segment-specific offer
    the giants are too big to bother copying — then build the switching costs that make
    your position defensible.
  </p>

  <h2>Segmentation: cutting the market at its joints</h2>
  <p>
    No product suits everyone, so the first act of marketing is <strong>segmentation</strong>:
    dividing a market into groups whose members would buy for the <em>same reason</em>. The
    classic bases are demographic (age, income, family stage), geographic (region, urban
    versus rural), psychographic (lifestyle, values) and behavioural (usage rate, loyalty,
    benefits sought) — but the cutting rule that matters is the first one: <strong>segments
    are discovered in why-questions, not age brackets</strong>. Toothpaste is the textbook
    case: one shelf serves at least three genuine segments — people buying
    <em>health/protection</em> (cavity prevention), people buying <em>appearance</em>
    (whitening, fresh breath), people buying <em>sensory pleasure</em> (flavour, texture).
    A 60-year-old and a 16-year-old may be in the same segment; two 35-year-olds may be in
    different ones. Segment properly and three things follow: products can be tailored to
    what each group actually values, prices can reflect it (the whitening premium is real),
    and advertising reaches the right eyes instead of being sprayed everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Targeting and positioning</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Targeting</strong> is the choice of which segments to serve — evaluated on the
    segment's size and growth, its accessibility (can you reach it profitably?), the
    strength of competition already in it, and the fit with what you're actually good at.
    Mass marketing serves everyone with one offer (utilities, table salt); niche marketing
    serves one segment deeply (specialist running shoes for overpronators); concentrated
    strategies dominate a small pond before trying a bigger one. <strong>Positioning</strong>
    then answers: when a customer compares options, which box do you sit in? The standard
    tool is a <strong>positioning map</strong> — two attributes customers care about (say,
    price versus quality) with every competitor plotted. Gaps on the map are either
    opportunity (unserved demand) or warning (no demand there — check before celebrating).
    Positioning lives or dies on <strong>differentiation</strong>: a unique selling point a
    competitor cannot cheaply copy. Budget airlines positioned on price and simplicity;
    full-service carriers on routes, lounges and flexibility — both profitable, in
    different boxes, with different cost structures to make the promise true. The test of
    a real position: a customer can finish the sentence "X is the one that ___", and
    competitors can't occupy the same slot credibly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> positioning is the tagline. The tagline is the last
    1 per cent of positioning; the first 99 is the <em>operations that make the claim
    true</em>. A budget airline that positions on "the lowest fare" must actually run
    single aircraft types, secondary airports, no-refund tickets and fast turnarounds —
    every cost decision exists to keep the promise cheap. A luxury brand claiming
    craftsmanship must actually constrain supply and sweat the finish. Positioning
    operations cannot support is a lie customers discover, and discovered lies become
    the positioning.
  </p>

  <h2>Market research: buying information before spending money</h2>
  <p>
    Research is how the guesses above become evidence. <strong>Primary research</strong> is
    gathered first-hand for this question — surveys, interviews, focus groups,
    observation, test markets — and splits into <em>quantitative</em> (numbers: how many,
    how much; survey a sample and project) and <em>qualitative</em> (reasons: why do they
    switch; interviews and observation). <strong>Secondary research</strong> reuses what
    exists — government statistics, industry reports, competitor accounts, search-volume
    data — cheaper and faster, but rarely answering exactly your question. Sampling brings
    its own traps: a <em>random</em> sample gives everyone equal chance (unbiased but
    expensive); <em>quota</em> sampling fills preset category quotas (cheap but only as
    good as the interviewer's judgement); <em>bias</em> enters through who answers
    (surveying your existing customers about whether to keep your existing product),
    through questions that suggest answers, and through people's reliable overstatement of
    their own future virtue. The cardinal rule of startup-era research fixes the last two:
    <strong>ask about the past, not the future</strong> — "tell me about the last time you
    solved this problem" yields evidence; "would you use an app that…" yields polite
    fiction. The professional framing of the market itself is the TAM/SAM/SOM stack:
    <em>total addressable</em> market (everyone with the problem), <em>serviceable</em>
    (the part you can reach with your channels and geography), <em>obtainable</em> (the
    share you could realistically win in a few years). Sizing honestly separates fundable
    ideas from wishful arithmetic — "1 per cent of China" is not a market size.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitative research carries its own arithmetic, worth knowing before commissioning
    anything. A random sample of size n estimates a proportion with a margin of error of
    roughly 1 ÷ √n: 400 respondents give about ±5 percentage points, 1,000 about ±3.2. So
    "68 per cent of our customers would pay more" from a 400-person sample really means
    "somewhere between 63 and 73" — and a product decision hinging on the difference
    between 68 and 71 per cent needs a bigger sample, not a bolder slide. Cost follows
    √n: doubling precision quadruples the sample. This is also why qualitative and
    quantitative work in pairs — interviews find the hypothesis worth testing; the survey
    finds out how widely it holds.
  </p>

  <h2>Product–market fit: the verdict</h2>
  <p>
    Marc Andreessen defined product–market fit as "the point where the market pulls the
    product out of you" — demand arrives faster than you can serve it, churn falls,
    word of mouth does the marketing budget's job. Before that point, startups die of
    premature scaling: hiring, spending and building for demand that hasn't shown up. The
    honest signals are behavioural rather than rhetorical. The best-known is
    <strong>Sean Ellis's 40 per cent test</strong>: survey active users with "how would you
    feel if you could no longer use this product?" — if fewer than about 40 per cent answer
    "very disappointed", the product is not yet essential to a core group, and the move is
    to refocus on whatever sub-group scores highest, not to advertise harder. Retention is
    the other tell: a product people return to without prompting has fit; one needing
    constant paid re-acquisition has a leak, whatever the launch numbers said. Fit is also
    <em>perishable</em> — platforms, tastes and competitors move, and yesterday's fit is
    today's churn.
  </p>

  <h2>Jobs to be done: the deeper segmentation</h2>
  <p>
    Clayton Christensen's refinement cuts beneath customer types to the situation:
    <strong>customers don't buy products, they hire them for a job that arises in their
    life</strong>. His famous case: a fast-food chain couldn't sell more milkshakes by
    improving them against other milkshakes — but watching <em>when</em> they were bought
    revealed the job: morning commuters wanted something to make a boring drive survivable,
    filling, one-handed, not another doughnut. The competing products were bagels, bananas
    and boredom — never listed as rivals by any category report. The method's power is that
    it reveals competitors the category never shows you (the film industry's job was
    "entertainment for an evening", so television — then streaming — was the competitor
    all along) and it explains purchases demographics can't: the same person "hires"
    different products for the same job in different moods. When segmentation and jobs
    disagree, jobs usually win.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four extensions. <strong>Network-effect markets break the normal rules:</strong> in
    marketplaces and social platforms, the product's value comes from other users, so
    winner-take-most dynamics apply — speed and initial liquidity matter more than features,
    and the sensible strategy (subsidise one side, charge the other; solve the cold-start
    problem city by city) is different from classic segment-and-position. <strong>Switching
    costs</strong> are the gravity of positioning: the more a customer has invested — data,
    habits, integrations, contracts — the stickier even a mediocre product is, which is why
    incumbents defend "ecosystems" and why fit must be re-earned when a platform shift
    resets everyone's switching costs to zero. <strong>Over-segmentation</strong> is the
    failure mode of enthusiastic researchers: forty micro-segments with no product or
    budget differences between them is analysis theatre; a segment earns its existence only
    if you would serve it <em>differently</em>. And <strong>the ethics line</strong> is
    worth drawing early: the same research tools that match products to needs can
    manipulate vulnerable people — dark patterns, predatory targeting, addiction-by-design.
    The line most frameworks draw is between helping a customer get what they already want
    and engineering what they want. The master skill underneath everything here is
    judgement under uncertainty: segments are hypotheses, research is evidence, and the
    mature marketer holds every conclusion loosely enough to update it when the market
    votes with its feet. The next lesson, Marketing Fundamentals, takes STP
    into tactics: the 4Ps, the funnel and lifetime value.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kotler &amp; Keller, Marketing Management; Christensen et al., Competing Against Luck; Andreessen, "The Only Thing That Matters" (2010); Ellis, "Startup Marketing" (2009–10).</p>
</TopicPage>
